[Gate analysis] — why the literal `xcodebuild -scheme TeslaSync build test` cannot include this
surface in THIS isolated slot, with evidence
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. The Xcode project is generated from apps/apple/project.yml (the .xcodeproj is git-ignored). The
   app targets list their sources explicitly:
       - path: TeslaSync/dashboard-widgets
       - path: TeslaSync/feature-views
   project.yml does NOT list `TeslaSync/shared-surfaces`. After `xcodegen generate`:
       grep -c DraftRestorePrompt TeslaSync.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj  -> 0   (surface EXCLUDED)
       grep -c AiUsageCard        TeslaSync.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj  -> 60  (feature-views IN)
   Wiring `shared-surfaces` into project.yml is OUTSIDE this prompt's allowed files
   ("Allowed files: apps/apple/TeslaSync/shared-surfaces/DraftRestorePrompt.*, the log file").
   It is a harness/build-config responsibility shared by all 117+ shared-surface prompts (editing
   it per-prompt would collide across the parallel slots).
2. The app build's pre-action stages a KMP `Shared.xcframework` (apps/shared Gradle). This IS
   feasible on this runner — an offline probe progressed through real Kotlin/Native tasks:
       :core:compileKotlinIosArm64 FROM-CACHE / :core:linkDebugFrameworkIosArm64 /
       :core:compileKotlinIosSimulatorArm64 FROM-CACHE ...  (cached K/N toolchain present)
   i.e. the framework is NOT the blocker; the project.yml wiring (item 1) is.

=> Because the surface is not in the target, an `xcodebuild build test` here would build the app
   WITHOUT this surface — it would neither compile nor exercise it, so reporting IOS_EXIT/MAC_EXIT=0
   from such a run would be a hollow green (Honesty Covenant rule 1: "No red-as-green"). Instead,
   the surface was verified by the equivalent app-settings compile + test above (Verify 1 & 2).

